.TH CYAML_LIST_PUSH 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_list_push \- append a child to a list
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

ccol_retval_t cyaml_list_push(cyaml seq, cyaml child);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Append child to the CYAML_LIST node seq.  On success, ownership of child
transfers to seq, so do not free child after such a call.

One return code carries one rule about child.  ccol_invalid_args always
means that the function rejected the arguments and did not touch child
at all: the caller still owns child and must destroy it itself.  Every
other failure takes ownership of child and deep-frees it before the
function returns, so the caller must not destroy child after any of
those failures.  The caller can therefore act on the return code alone,
and that is the only way to act on one code at all.

The function rejects with ccol_invalid_args a NULL child, a NULL seq, a
seq that is not a CYAML_LIST, a child that is already attached to a
list parent or a dictionary parent, and a child that is seq itself.  A
child that is already attached would have two owners if the function
accepted it, and each owner frees the node when its own parent is
destroyed.  A node that you just created is always acceptable, and so
is a fresh cyaml_clone().  A borrowed reference from cyaml_get(),
cyaml_list_get() or cyaml_dictionary_get() is never acceptable, because
each of those names a node that already has a parent.

child must also not already hold seq somewhere in its own subtree, and
the function does not check this.  A cycle that you make this way turns
a later cyaml_destroy(), cyaml_clone() or cyaml_serialize() call on the
tree into undefined behaviour.  The attachment rule above excludes this
case for every node that has a parent; what stays unchecked is an
ancestor with no parent, which is the root of the tree that seq belongs
to.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
seq    CYAML_LIST target node.
child  Node to append.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success, ccol_invalid_args, ccol_not_enough_memory, or
ccol_container_full.
ccol_invalid_args leaves child untouched, and the caller still owns it.
It comes back when seq or child is NULL, when seq is not a list, when
child is already attached, or when child is seq itself.
ccol_container_full means that the list holds the largest element count
that it can represent, which a real program does not reach.
Ownership of child transfers on every outcome other than
ccol_invalid_args.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_list_get (3),
.BR cyaml_list_len (3),
.BR cyaml_create_list (3)
